The ozone hole is not simply a large region with "less ozone." It is the result of an exceptional interaction between atmospheric chemistry and Antarctic meteorology. Extremely cold temperatures generate polar stratospheric clouds, while the polar vortex isolates the air mass. Reactions on these clouds convert relatively inactive chlorine compounds into highly reactive forms; sunlight returning in Antarctic spring then enables catalytic chlorine/bromine cycles that rapidly destroy ozone. The modern ozone problem, however, extends far beyond this single mechanism. It now encompasses uncontrolled feedstocks, very short-lived substances, the climate-ozone coupling, and the potential impacts of future climate interventions.

