Chapter 2 — The Anthropogenic Ozone Crisis

2.1–2.5 The Ozone-Depleting Substances

The primary anthropogenic ozone-depleting substances (ODS) are:

Substance Primary Use Atmospheric Lifetime
CFC-11 (CCl₃F) Refrigeration, foams ~52 years
CFC-12 (CCl₂F₂) Refrigeration, aerosols ~102 years
Halon-1301 (CBrF₃) Fire suppression ~65 years
Carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄) Solvent, feedstock ~33 years
Methyl chloroform (CH₃CCl₃) Solvent ~5 years
Methyl bromide (CH₃Br) Fumigant ~0.7 years

2.6 Long Atmospheric Lifetimes: The Core Problem

A central concept:

 \boxed{ \text{Emission} \rightarrow \text{Atmospheric transport} \rightarrow \text{Stratospheric conversion} \rightarrow \text{Reactive halogens} \rightarrow \text{Ozone destruction} } 

Because CFCs and halons are chemically inert in the troposphere, they survive long enough to be transported into the stratosphere. Only there, exposed to intense UV radiation, do they photolyze and release reactive chlorine and bromine atoms. The problem is therefore spatially and temporally separated from its original source. This decoupling has profound implications for governance: emissions from a refrigerator in one hemisphere eventually contribute to ozone destruction over the other.

2.7 Very Short-Lived Substances (VSLSs)

The 2022 WMO/UNEP Assessment highlights that tropospheric chlorine from Very Short-Lived Substances (VSLSs), notably dichloromethane (CH₂Cl₂), increased by 2.1 ± 0.6 ppt Cl yr⁻¹ during 2016–2020, contributing 4% of total stratospheric chlorine input in 2020. These compounds are not controlled by the Montreal Protocol, their emissions are growing (primarily in Asia), and they represent a leakage path in the ozone protection regime. Ignoring VSLSs in any modern assessment is a serious scientific omission.

2.8 Nitrous Oxide (N₂O) as an Ozone-Depleting Substance

The most recent assessment makes a decisive point: anthropogenic N₂O emissions in 2020, expressed as a CFC-11-equivalent, were more than two times the ODP-weighted emissions from all CFCs in that year. N₂O is the most abundant ODS today by ODP-weighted emissions. It is not controlled by the Montreal Protocol and is tightly coupled to agricultural practices, making it a critical component of the modern ozone problem.

