Chapter 3 — Why the Ozone Hole Appears Above Antarctica

3.1 The Antarctic Polar Vortex

During the Southern Hemisphere winter, the strong temperature gradient between the pole and mid-latitudes drives a powerful circumpolar jet stream. This creates an isolated air mass—the polar vortex—that prevents mixing with warmer, ozone-rich air from lower latitudes.

3.2 Extreme Stratospheric Cooling

Within the vortex, radiative cooling during the polar night drives temperatures to extreme lows. Antarctic stratospheric temperatures regularly fall below −78°C (195 K), far colder than Arctic conditions.

3.3 Polar Stratospheric Clouds

Below approximately −78°C, water vapor and nitric acid condense to form polar stratospheric clouds (PSCs). Two primary types are distinguished:

· Type I (NAT): Composed of nitric acid trihydrate, forming near 195 K. They enable chlorine activation and denitrification via sedimentation.
· Type II (Ice): Forming near 188 K, these cause severe denitrification and dehydration.

These clouds provide surfaces for heterogeneous chemical reactions that would not occur in the gas phase.

3.4 HCl and ClONO₂ Activation and Denitrification

The key heterogeneous reactions on PSC surfaces convert reservoir species into reactive species:

 HCl + ClONO_2 \xrightarrow{\text{PSC surface}} Cl_2 + HNO_3 
 Cl_2 + h\nu \rightarrow 2Cl 

This is the crucial activation step. Additionally, PSC sedimentation removes HNO₃ (denitrification), preventing NO₂ from re-forming ClONO₂ and deactivating ClO. Without denitrification, the catalytic cycle would be much shorter-lived. This explains why the Antarctic hole is so much deeper and more persistent than Arctic depletion.

3.5 ClO and BrO Chemistry

Once sunlight returns in Antarctic spring, the reactive chlorine drives catalytic destruction. The dominant cycle involves the ClO dimer:

 ClO + ClO + M \rightarrow Cl_2O_2 + M 
 Cl_2O_2 + h\nu \rightarrow Cl + ClO_2 
 ClO_2 + M \rightarrow Cl + O_2 + M 
 2(Cl + O_3 \rightarrow ClO + O_2) 
Net:  2O_3 \rightarrow 3O_2 

The ClO–BrO cycle is particularly efficient:

 ClO + BrO \rightarrow Cl + Br + O_2 
 Cl + O_3 \rightarrow ClO + O_2 
 Br + O_3 \rightarrow BrO + O_2 
Net:  2O_3 \rightarrow 3O_2 

A single chlorine atom can participate in thousands of catalytic cycles, destroying on the order of 10⁵ ozone molecules before being deactivated by reaction with methane or nitrogen dioxide.

3.6 Spring Sunlight and 3.7 Catalytic Destruction

Sunlight returning to the Antarctic in September–October photolyzes  Cl_2 , releasing atomic chlorine and initiating the catalytic cycles. This is why the ozone hole appears in Antarctic spring, despite ozone-depleting conditions persisting through winter. The "ozone hole" is conventionally defined as the region where TCO falls below 220 DU.

