Chapter 6 — The Montreal Protocol as a Control System: Successes, Stress Tests, and Loopholes

This interlude bridges historical success with the modern systemic challenges that define the need for this thesis.

6.1 The CFC-11 Case: A Stress Test of Enforcement

Atmospheric monitoring detected an unexpected, persistent rise in global CFC-11 emissions after 2012. Inverse modeling from Korean and Japanese stations traced 40–60% of the global increase to eastern China, indicating new, undeclared production. Emissions declined after 2018 following international pressure, but the episode revealed gaps in the observing network and the power of atmospheric monitoring to detect non-compliance. It establishes a real-world precedent for the AI-based emission detection architecture proposed in Part II of this thesis.

6.2 The Feedstock Loophole: A Control-System Boundary Failure

The April 2026 MIT-led Nature Communications paper by Reimann et al. fundamentally transforms our understanding of residual emissions. The key findings are:

· ODS used as feedstocks (to make plastics, nonstick chemicals, and HFC/HFO substitutes) are exempt from Montreal Protocol controls.
· Industry assumed a leakage rate of 0.5%; AGAGE measurements show an actual rate of 3.6%.
· Feedstock production has increased 75% by mass over the last decade.
· At this leakage rate, mid-latitude ozone recovery is delayed from 2066 to 2073 (a 7-year delay; uncertainty 6–11 years).
· Eliminating feedstock emissions would advance the return of stratospheric chlorine to pre-1980 levels by almost 4 years, largely due to carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄).

This is a systems-engineering failure. The Protocol controls consumption and production for emissive use but not production for chemical transformation. This is a boundary condition error in the global control system.

6.3 The 17-Year Delay in Recovery Projections

The projected return date for Equivalent Effective Stratospheric Chlorine (EESC) to pre-1980 levels has slipped from 2049 (in the 2006 Assessment) to 2066 (in the 2022 Assessment)—a 17-year delay. Causes include revised atmospheric lifetimes, larger-than-expected banks, unanticipated emissions (e.g., the CFC-11 spike), and feedstock growth. This illustrates how model uncertainty and policy gaps compound over time, a core theme for an engineering theory of planetary recovery.

6.4 The Legacy Bank Problem

The "bank" refers to ODS still contained in existing equipment, insulation foams, and landfills. These will eventually leak into the atmosphere. The 2022 Assessment notes that the projected return of mid-latitude EESC is delayed by 6 years compared to the previous assessment, mostly due to larger assessed banks. Banks are a hidden state variable in the system dynamics.

