Chapter 7 — Why Recovery Is Slow: A Systems Dynamics Perspective

Even after emissions decline, the atmospheric system does not immediately return to its previous state.

7.1 Atmospheric Inertia and the Three-Category Delay Framework

The delay mechanisms can be structured into three categories:

1. Atmospheric Inertia: Long-lived CFCs and halons already in the atmosphere; slow Brewer-Dobson circulation transport timescales (3–5 years from tropics to mid-latitudes, 5–7 years to poles).
2. Legacy Banks: Chemicals in old equipment, insulation, and landfills that will eventually leak.
3. Active Production Loopholes: Feedstocks, by-products, and VSLSs that are uncontrolled or under-controlled.

The key concept becomes:

 \boxed{ \text{Emission reduction} \neq \text{Immediate atmospheric recovery} } 

