Appendix B: Catalytic Destruction Cycles and the Ozone Loss Rate

B.1 Generic Catalytic Cycle

The general form of a catalytic ozone destruction cycle is:

 X + O_3 \rightarrow XO + O_2 
 XO + O \rightarrow X + O_2 
Net:  O_3 + O \rightarrow 2O_2 

The ozone loss rate due to catalyst  X  is:

 L_{O_3}^{(X)} = 2 k_{X+O_3} [X][O_3] 

The factor of 2 accounts for the fact that each cycle removes one  O_3  molecule directly (first step) and one  O  atom that would otherwise recombine to form ozone (second step).

B.2 The ClO Dimer Cycle (Antarctic)

The dominant catalytic cycle in the Antarctic ozone hole involves the ClO dimer:

 ClO + ClO + M \xrightarrow{k_{dimer}} Cl_2O_2 + M 
 Cl_2O_2 + h\nu \xrightarrow{J_{dimer}} Cl + ClO_2 
 ClO_2 + M \rightarrow Cl + O_2 + M 
 2(Cl + O_3 \rightarrow ClO + O_2) 
Net:  2O_3 \rightarrow 3O_2 

The loss rate is proportional to  [ClO]^2 :

 L_{O_3}^{(ClO)} = 2 J_{dimer} [Cl_2O_2] \propto [ClO]^2 

This quadratic dependence is why the ozone hole is so explosive: as ClO concentration builds, the loss rate increases dramatically.

B.3 The ClO–BrO Synergistic Cycle

The ClO–BrO cycle is more efficient than the ClO dimer cycle because it does not require the formation of a weakly bound dimer:

 ClO + BrO \rightarrow Cl + Br + O_2 
 Cl + O_3 \rightarrow ClO + O_2 
 Br + O_3 \rightarrow BrO + O_2 
Net:  2O_3 \rightarrow 3O_2 

This cycle is first-order in both  [ClO]  and  [BrO] , making it particularly important at the edges of the ozone hole and in the Arctic.

B.4 Quantification of Catalytic Efficiency

A single chlorine atom can destroy on the order of  10^5  ozone molecules before being deactivated by reaction with methane ( CH_4 ) or nitrogen dioxide ( NO_2 ):

 Cl + CH_4 \rightarrow HCl + CH_3 
 ClO + NO_2 + M \rightarrow ClONO_2 + M 

The catalytic chain length  L  is defined as:

 L = \frac{k_{Cl+O_3}[O_3]}{k_{Cl+CH_4}[CH_4] + k_{ClO+NO_2}[NO_2]} 

In the Antarctic vortex,  L \sim 10^5 . The distinction between per-pass efficiency (hundreds of ozone molecules) and integrated lifetime impact (tens of thousands) is crucial for scientific precision.

