Appendix C: Heterogeneous Chemistry on Polar Stratospheric Clouds

C.1 PSC Formation Thermodynamics

Polar Stratospheric Clouds form when stratospheric temperatures fall below the condensation threshold for nitric acid trihydrate (NAT) or water ice.

Type I (NAT) threshold:
 T_{NAT} \approx 195 \text{ K}  (at typical stratospheric HNO₃ and H₂O mixing ratios)

Type II (Ice) threshold:
 T_{Ice} \approx 188 \text{ K} 

The formation of NAT is governed by the equilibrium:

 HNO_3(g) + 3H_2O(s/l) \rightleftharpoons HNO_3 \cdot 3H_2O(s) 

C.2 Key Heterogeneous Reactions

On PSC surfaces, the following reactions occur:

Reaction 1:
 HCl(ads) + ClONO_2(g) \rightarrow Cl_2(g) + HNO_3(ads) 

Reaction 2:
 H_2O(ads) + ClONO_2(g) \rightarrow HOCl(g) + HNO_3(ads) 

Reaction 3:
 HCl(ads) + HOCl(g) \rightarrow Cl_2(g) + H_2O(ads) 

Reaction 4 (Hydrolysis of N₂O₅):
 N_2O_5(g) + H_2O(ads) \rightarrow 2HNO_3(ads) 

These reactions convert inert chlorine reservoirs ( HCl ,  ClONO_2 ) into photolabile species ( Cl_2 ,  HOCl ), which are readily photolyzed when sunlight returns in spring.

C.3 Denitrification and Dehydration

PSC particles can grow large enough to sediment out of the stratosphere, removing HNO₃ and H₂O. This process:

1. Permanently removes nitrogen oxides (NO_x) from the vortex air mass, preventing the deactivation of ClO into ClONO₂.
2. Dehydrates the stratosphere, suppressing future PSC formation.

The timescale for PSC sedimentation is days to weeks for particles reaching radii > 1 μm.

