Appendix G: The Climate–Ozone Coupling (Detailed)

G.1 Stratospheric Cooling

Greenhouse gas increases warm the troposphere but cool the stratosphere due to:

1. Enhanced infrared emission to space from stratospheric CO₂,
2. Reduced upwelling of warm tropospheric air (Brewer-Dobson circulation changes),
3. Stratospheric ozone depletion itself (which reduces stratospheric heating).

The cooling trend is approximately -0.5 to -1.0 K/decade in the lower stratosphere.

Effect on gas-phase chemistry: Lower temperatures slow the rate coefficients for catalytic ozone destruction cycles (e.g., the Chapman mechanism and HO_x cycles), which have positive activation energies. This is favorable for global ozone recovery.

Effect on polar chemistry: Lower temperatures increase the frequency and extent of PSC formation, enhancing heterogeneous chlorine activation and prolonging ozone depletion in polar regions.

G.2 Stratospheric Water Vapor Trends

Stratospheric water vapor increased by ~1 ppmv between 1980 and 2000 (the "tape recorder" signal), but has shown complex behavior since. Higher water vapor:

· Increases HO_x radical production:  H_2O + O(^1D) \rightarrow 2OH 
· Contributes to PSC formation at higher temperatures,
· Can enhance ozone destruction in the lower stratosphere.

G.3 Brewer-Dobson Circulation Acceleration

Climate models project an acceleration of the Brewer-Dobson circulation (BDC) in response to greenhouse gas forcing. This would:

· Transport more ozone from the tropics to mid-latitudes (increasing mid-latitude ozone),
· Reduce tropical ozone (where production is highest),
· Transport more ODS into the stratosphere (partially offsetting emission reductions).

The net effect on total ozone recovery is uncertain and model-dependent.

