Appendix H: Stratospheric Aerosol Injection (SAI) and Ozone

H.1 SAI Mechanisms and Ozone Impacts

Stratospheric Aerosol Injection (SAI) involves deliberately injecting sulfate aerosols (or other materials) into the stratosphere to reflect solar radiation and cool the Earth. The 2022 WMO/UNEP Assessment dedicated Chapter 6 to SAI for the first time.

Key findings on ozone impacts:

SAI Scenario Antarctic Ozone Response
Strong injection (e.g., 10–20 Mt S/yr) Ozone hole deepens, recovery delayed 25–50 years
Medium injection (e.g., 5 Mt S/yr) TCO reduced by 9–29 DU
Injection material: Sulfate (SO₂) More ozone loss than calcite or other materials
Injection latitude: Tropical Maximizes global cooling but may increase polar ozone loss via PSC enhancement

Mechanisms:

1. Heterogeneous chemistry: Sulfate aerosols provide surfaces for heterogeneous reactions, activating chlorine and bromine (similar to PSCs but at higher temperatures).
2. Dynamical effects: Aerosol heating changes stratospheric circulation, affecting ozone transport.
3. Radiation changes: Altered UV fluxes affect ozone photolysis rates.

H.2 Knowledge Gaps

The 2022 Assessment states: "Many knowledge gaps and uncertainties prevent a more robust evaluation at this time." These include:

· Aerosol microphysics (particle size, composition, evolution),
· Interactions with PSCs and polar chemistry,
· Long-term dynamical feedbacks,
· Regional and seasonal variability.

