Appendix J: Statistical Methods for Trend Detection

J.1 Multiple Linear Regression (MLR)

The standard method for detecting long-term ozone trends is MLR, which accounts for natural variability:

 O_3(t) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 t + \beta_2 QBO(t) + \beta_3 Solar(t) + \beta_4 ENSO(t) + \beta_5 Volcano(t) + \epsilon(t) 

where  \beta_1  is the long-term trend, and the other terms represent natural forcings.

Limitations:

· Assumes linear superposition of forcings,
· Sensitive to model specification,
· Does not capture non-linear interactions.

J.2 Dynamical Linear Modeling (DLM)

DLM is a state-space approach that allows the trend  \beta_1(t)  to vary in time:

 O_3(t) = \beta_0(t) + \beta_1(t) t + \sum \beta_i X_i(t) + \epsilon(t) 

This is more flexible but requires careful Bayesian inference and is computationally expensive.

J.3 Attribution Methods

Detection and attribution studies compare observed trends with model-simulated trends under different forcing scenarios. Formal detection is achieved when the observed trend is statistically distinguishable from natural variability at a specified confidence level (typically 95%).

